{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully.\n\nThe task SE-PE-01 requires implementing a `solve(case)` function that:\n1. Takes a payload with `nodes` (a tree structure) and `required_symbols`\n2. Returns `removed_node_ids` and `retained_node_ids`\n\nLooking at the fixture cases:\n\nEach node has:\n- `id`: string identifier\n- `parent`: parent node id (null for root)\n- `symbol`: a symbol string\n- `depth`: depth in tree\n- `removable`: boolean\n- `utility`: float value\n\n`required_symbols`: list of symbols that must be retained\n\nThe task seems to be about pruning a tree while keeping nodes with required symbols.\n\nLet me think about the logic:\n\n1. Nodes with `removable: false` cannot be removed\n2. Required symbols must be present in the retained set\n3. We need to determine which nodes to remove and which to retain\n\nThe key constraint seems to be:\n- A node can only be removed if it's `removable: true`\n- Nodes whose symbol is in `required_symbols` must be retained\n- If a parent is removed, all its children must also be removed (they become orphaned)\n\nWait, let me re-read more carefully. The task is \"SE-PE-01\" - \"SE\" might stand for \"Symbol Elimination\" or \"Subtree Elimination\", \"PE\" might stand for \"Pruning Elimination\".\n\nLooking at the cases:\n- `required_symbols` includes \"root\" and \"s1\"\n- Nodes with those symbols must be retained\n- Non-removable nodes must be retained\n\nLet me think about what makes sense:\n1. A node is retained if:\n   - It is not removable, OR\n   - Its symbol is in required_symbols, OR\n   - It has a descendant that must be retained (i.e., we need to keep the path)\n\n2. A node is removed if:\n   - It is removable AND\n   - Its symbol is not in required_symbols AND\n   - None of its descendants need to be retained\n\nWait, but if a parent is removed, its children are also removed (implicitly). Let me think about this differently.\n\nActually, the tree pruning problem: we want to find the minimal subtree that:\n1. Includes all non-removable nodes\n2. Includes all nodes with symbols in required_symbols\n3. Includes all ancestors of the above nodes (to maintain connectivity)\n\nBut removable nodes can be removed even if their children must be retained... no, that doesn't make sense in a tree. If you remove a node, its subtree is cut off.\n\nLet me reconsider. The constraint might be:\n- A node is removed if it's `removable=true` AND we choose to remove it\n- When a node is removed, its entire subtree is also removed\n- We cannot remove a node that is `removable=false`\n- We must ensure all `required_symbols` are still represented in the tree\n\nSo the question becomes: which removable nodes can we remove without losing coverage of required_symbols?\n\nActually wait - let me think again. If we remove n2 (removable=true, symbol=s2), then n4 (child of n2, removable=true, symbol=s1) is also removed. But s1 is a required symbol! However, n1 also has symbol s1 and n1 is not removable. So it's okay to remove n2 (and n4) because s1 is still covered by n1.\n\nSo the algorithm might be:\n1. For each removable node, check if removing it (and its subtree) would eliminate all instances of any required symbol\n2. If removing a subtree would eliminate a required symbol that has no other representation, don't remove it\n3. Otherwise, remove it (maximize removal? or minimize?)\n\nActually, I think the task is specifically about determining which nodes CAN be removed vs must be retained, given:\n- Non-removable nodes are always retained\n- Removable nodes are removed if their subtree doesn't contain any node that is either non-removable or provides coverage for a required symbol that isn't covered elsewhere\n\nLet me think about this more carefully with case 000:\n\nNodes:\n- root (depth 0, not removable, symbol=root) → required\n- n1 (depth 1, not removable, symbol=s1) → s1 is required\n- n2 (depth 1, removable, symbol=s2) → s2 not required\n- n3 (depth 1, not removable, symbol=s0) → not required but not removable\n- n4 (depth 2, removable, symbol=s1, parent=n2) → s1 is required, but n1 also covers s1\n- n5 (depth 2, not removable, symbol=s2, parent=n3) → not required but not removable\n- n6 (depth 2, removable, symbol=s0, parent=n1) → s0 not required\n\nSo:\n- root: retained (not removable)\n- n1: retained (not removable)\n- n3: retained (not removable)\n- n5: retained (not removable, child of n3)\n- n2: removable, symbol=s2 not required. Its subtree has n4 (s1, but s1 is covered by n1). So n2 can be removed → n4 also removed\n- n6: removable, symbol=s0, s0 not required. No children. Can be removed.\n\nSo: removed = [n2, n4, n6], retained = [root, n1, n3, n5]\n\nThe logic seems to be:\n1. Mark all non-removable nodes as \"must retain\"\n2. For removable nodes, check if the subtree rooted at that node contains any \"must retain\" node or any node with a required symbol that isn't covered elsewhere\n3. If a removable node's subtree doesn't contribute anything essential, remove the entire subtree\n\nBut \"covered elsewhere\" is the tricky part. Let me think about it as:\n\nA required symbol is \"covered\" if there exists at least one node with that symbol that is either not removable or is in a subtree that won't be removed.\n\nActually I think the algorithm is simpler:\n\nFor a subtree rooted at a removable node X:\n- If every node in the subtree is either removable OR (removable=false but has no required_symbol coverage that isn't available outside the subtree)... \n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me think differently.\n\nPerhaps the rule is: a removable node is removed if and only if it has no descendants that are non-removable or that have symbols in required_symbols.\n\nWait, but what if a required_symbol is in a removable node that is also a descendant of a removable node? Then removing the ancestor removes the required symbol.\n\nLet me try a different approach:\n\nA node is \"essential\" if:\n1. It is not removable, OR\n2. Its symbol is in required_symbols AND no other node with the same symbol exists that will be retained\n\nActually I think the simplest interpretation might be:\n\nA removable node is removed if its subtree contains no non-removable nodes AND contains no nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols (or all such symbols are covered by nodes outside this subtree).\n\nBut circular dependency here. Let me try top-down greedy:\n\nProcess nodes top-down. A node is removed if:\n1. It is removable\n2. Its symbol is not in required_symbols\n3. None of its descendants are \"needed\" (i.e., non-removable or have required symbols that aren't covered elsewhere)\n\nFor case 000:\n- n2 is removable, symbol=s2 (not required). Its subtree: n4 (removable, symbol=s1, required). But s1 is also covered by n1 (not removable). So n2's subtree has a \"required\" symbol but it's covered elsewhere. Can we remove n2?\n\nIf yes, then n2 and n4 are removed.\n\n- n6 is removable, symbol=s0 (not required). No descendants. Removed.\n\nSo removed = [n2, n4, n6], retained = [root, n1, n3, n5]\n\nFor case 001: same structure, same symbols, so same result.\n\nFor case 002: same structure, same symbols, so same result.\n\nNow let me think about the algorithm more carefully:\n\nStep 1: Build a mapping of symbol → list of nodes with that symbol\nStep 2: Identify which symbols are \"required\"\nStep 3: For each required symbol, find all nodes that have that symbol\nStep 4: A node with a required symbol is \"pinned\" if it's the only node (or the only non-removable node?) with that required symbol\n\nActually, I think the algorithm might be:\n\n1. First, determine which nodes MUST be retained:\n   - All non-removable nodes\n   - All nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols AND has no alternative (i.e., all other nodes with that symbol are removable and would be removed)\n\n2. A removable node is removed if none of its descendants are \"must retain\"\n\nBut this has circular dependency. Let me think about it differently.\n\nBottom-up approach:\n1. Mark all non-removable nodes as \"keep\"\n2. For each required symbol, find all nodes with that symbol. If ALL of them are removable, then we need to keep at least one.\n3. For removable nodes, mark as \"keep\" if they have any descendant that is \"keep\"\n4. Otherwise, removable nodes are \"removed\"\n\nWait, but what if a required symbol appears in a removable node but there are non-removable nodes with the same symbol too? Then the removable node doesn't need to be kept for that symbol.\n\nLet me formalize:\n\nA node is \"required to keep\" (RTK) if:\n1. It is not removable, OR\n2. It is removable AND its symbol is in required_symbols AND all other nodes with that symbol are going to be removed\n\nThen a removable node is retained if any of its descendants is RTK... but again circular.\n\nI think the cleaner approach:\n\n1. Compute \"covered symbols\" by non-removable nodes (or nodes that will definitely be retained)\n2. A removable node must be retained if its symbol is required and not covered by any non-removable node\n\nBut \"not covered by any non-removable node\" is key. If a required symbol has at least one non-removable node, then all removable nodes with that symbol can be removed.\n\nLet me try this:\n\n```\nrequired_by_non_removable = {node.symbol for node in nodes if not node.removable and node.symbol in required_symbols}\n```\n\nA required symbol is \"safe\" (covered by non-removable nodes) if it's in required_by_non_removable.\n\nA removable node is \"pinned\" if its symbol is in required_symbols and NOT in required_by_non_removable.\n\nThen:\n- A subtree rooted at a removable node is removed if no node in the subtree is non-removable OR pinned.\n\nBut wait - even if a node in the subtree is non-removable, it still gets cut off if its ancestor is removed... unless we can't remove non-removable nodes.\n\nHmm! The constraint is that we can't remove non-removable nodes. So if a non-removable node is a descendant of a removable node, we cannot remove that removable node!\n\nThis changes things. Let me re-examine case 000:\n- n2 (removable) → n4 (removable). Can we remove n2? Yes, because n4 is removable (and n5 is non-removable but is a child of n3, not n2).\n- n3 (not removable) → n5 (not removable). Can't remove n3.\n- n6 (removable, leaf). Can remove n6.\n\nSo the rule for removing a subtree rooted at removable node X:\n- All descendants of X that are non-removable must not exist (otherwise we'd be forcibly removing non-removable nodes)\n- OR: we can only remove the subtree if ALL nodes in the subtree (including X) are removable\n\nWait, that's a better interpretation:\n- We can only remove node X if X is removable AND all descendants of X that would also be removed are either removable themselves OR not in X's subtree (already orphaned by a higher removal).\n\nActually the simplest rule might be:\n- A removable node X is removed if all nodes in its subtree (including X) are removable AND the removal of this subtree doesn't eliminate all instances of any required symbol.\n\nLet me check case 000 with n2:\n- n2 subtree: {n2, n4} - both removable ✓\n- required symbols: {root, s1}\n  - root: covered by root node (not in n2's subtree)\n  - s1: covered by n1 (not removable, not in n2's subtree) ✓\n- So we can remove n2 and n4.\n\nn6:\n- subtree: {n6} - removable ✓  \n- s0 not required ✓\n- Remove n6.\n\nThis all makes sense. The algorithm:\n\n1. Build subtree for each node\n2. For a removable node X, it can be removed if:\n   a. All nodes in X's subtree are removable\n   b. For each required symbol S, there exists at least one node with symbol S outside X's subtree\n\n3. We want to maximize removals (greedy: remove if possible)\n\nBut we need to be careful about order. If removing X makes it possible to remove more nodes (e.g., X's parent), we should process top-down.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. Since we're dealing with a tree:\n- If we remove a node, its entire subtree is also removed\n- We want to remove as many nodes as possible (or just identify which can be removed)\n- Constraint: can't remove non-removable nodes, and must maintain at least one instance of each required symbol\n\nThe greedy approach: process nodes top-down. For each removable node, check if we can remove its subtree (all removable + required symbols still covered). If yes, remove it. If no, keep it and recurse into children.\n\nFor case 000:\n- root: not removable, keep\n  - n1: not removable, keep\n    - n6: removable. Subtree = {n6}, all removable. Required symbols: root (covered by root), s1 (covered by n1). Can remove n6. REMOVE.\n  - n2: removable. Subtree = {n2, n4}, all removable. Required: root (covered), s1 (covered by n1). REMOVE n2 and n4.\n  - n3: not removable, keep\n    - n5: not removable, keep\n\nResult: removed = [n2, n4, n6], retained = [root, n1, n3, n5]\n\nNow let me code this up:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    \n    # Build node lookup and tree structure\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    root_id = None\n    \n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n        else:\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\n    \n    # For each node, compute the set of symbols in its subtree\n    def get_subtree_ids(node_id):\n        result = [node_id]\n        for child_id in children[node_id]:\n            result.extend(get_subtree_ids(child_id))\n        return result\n    \n    # Count how many nodes have each required symbol (globally)\n    from collections import defaultdict\n    symbol_count = defaultdict(list)\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            symbol_count[n['symbol']].append(n['id'])\n    \n    removed = set()\n    \n    def can_remove_subtree(node_id):\n        \"\"\"Check if the entire subtree rooted at node_id can be removed.\"\"\"\n        subtree = get_subtree_ids(node_id)\n        # All nodes in subtree must be removable\n        for nid in subtree:\n            if not node_map[nid]['removable']:\n                return False\n        # Must not eliminate all instances of any required symbol\n        subtree_set = set(subtree)\n        for sym, nodes_with_sym in symbol_count.items():\n            # Nodes with this symbol outside this subtree (and not already removed)\n            outside = [nid for nid in nodes_with_sym if nid not in subtree_set and nid not in removed]\n            if len(outside) == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n    \n    def prune(node_id):\n        node = node_map[node_id]\n        if node['removable'] and can_remove_subtree(node_id):\n            subtree = get_subtree_ids(node_id)\n            for nid in subtree:\n                removed.add(nid)\n        else:\n            for child_id in children[node_id]:\n                prune(child_id)\n    \n    prune(root_id)\n    \n    all_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes]\n    removed_list = [nid for nid in all_ids if nid in removed]\n    retained_list = [nid for nid in all_ids if nid not in removed]\n    \n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_list,\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_list\n    }\n```\n\nWait, but there's an issue with my `can_remove_subtree` check - I'm looking at `symbol_count` globally, but I should consider what's outside the current subtree AND not already removed.\n\nActually, since we process top-down and once we decide to remove a subtree we mark it removed, we need to make sure the remaining nodes (not removed) still cover required symbols.\n\nLet me reconsider: when processing node X top-down:\n- \"outside\" means nodes not in X's subtree\n- We haven't processed X's siblings yet, but they are still present\n\nThe greedy approach processes top-down, and once we remove a subtree, its nodes are marked removed. When we check later nodes, we don't need to worry about already-removed nodes.\n\nBut actually, for the `can_remove_subtree` check, I should look at whether required symbols are covered by nodes NOT in the current subtree (regardless of whether those outside nodes are later removed - we can deal with that constraint when we process those nodes).\n\nActually no - if node A's subtree contains some required symbol, and we're deciding whether to remove node B's subtree which also has that required symbol, we should consider: will node A's subtree remain? Only if we don't remove it too.\n\nThis is getting complex. For simplicity and correctness, let me take a different approach:\n\nCompute the \"minimal required set\" first:\n1. All non-removable nodes must be retained\n2. For each required symbol, if there's at least one non-removable node with that symbol, then all removable nodes with that symbol can potentially be removed\n3. For each required symbol with NO non-removable nodes, we must retain at least one node with that symbol (the \"best\" one by some criterion, or the one that is already forced to be retained because its ancestors are retained)\n\nActually, for this problem I think the approach is:\n\n1. Determine \"forced retain\" nodes: all non-removable nodes\n2. Check which required symbols are NOT covered by forced-retain nodes → these need at least one representative\n3. For uncovered required symbols, find the best removable node to keep (but this gets complex)\n\nLooking at the fixtures, all `required_symbols` seem to always have coverage by non-removable nodes:\n- \"root\" → root node is not removable\n- \"s1\" → n1 is not removable and has symbol s1\n\nSo in all three cases, all required symbols are covered by non-removable nodes. This means all removable nodes CAN potentially be removed.\n\nAnd the only constraint on removal is: can't remove non-removable nodes.\n\nA removable node can be removed if ALL nodes in its subtree are removable (otherwise removing it would forcibly remove non-removable descendants).\n\nLet me check: in all cases, n2's subtree is {n2, n4} - both removable. n6 is a leaf and removable. So all removable nodes without non-removable descendants can be removed.\n\nBut wait - do we always want to maximize removal? The task might just be asking us to identify which nodes are \"removed\" according to some defined rule.\n\nGiven my analysis, I think the rule is:\n- A removable node is removed if its entire subtree consists of removable nodes (and the required symbols remain covered)\n- Since in the fixtures all required symbols are covered by non-removable nodes, the second condition is always satisfied\n\nLet me simplify: Remove a removable node if and only if all its descendants are removable (so we can remove the entire subtree without forcing removal of non-removable nodes), AND removing this subtree doesn't eliminate all coverage of any required symbol.\n\nFor the given cases, this would mean:\n- n2: removable, descendants = {n4} (removable). s1 is still covered by n1. Remove.\n- n6: removable, no descendants. Remove.\n- n4: descendant of n2, removed with n2.\n\nSo removed = [n2, n4, n6], retained = [root, n1, n3, n5] for all three cases (since the structure is the same).\n\nLet me write cleaner code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    \n    # Build lookup\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    root_id = None\n    \n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n        else:\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\n    \n    # Get all node IDs in the subtree rooted at node_id\n    def subtree_ids(node_id):\n        result = [node_id]\n        for cid in children[node_id]:\n            result.extend(subtree_ids(cid))\n        return result\n    \n    # Check if all nodes in subtree are removable\n    def all_removable(node_id):\n        if not node_map[node_id]['removable']:\n            return False\n        return all(all_removable(cid) for cid in children[node_id])\n    \n    # For required symbols, count nodes with that symbol outside a given subtree\n    def required_symbols_covered_outside(subtree_set):\n        for sym in required_symbols:\n            covered = any(\n                n['symbol'] == sym and n['id'] not in subtree_set\n                for n in nodes\n            )\n            if not covered:\n                return False\n        return True\n    \n    removed = set()\n    \n    def prune(node_id):\n        if node_map[node_id]['removable'] and all_removable(node_id):\n            sub = set(subtree_ids(node_id))\n            if required_symbols_covered_outside(sub):\n                removed.update(sub)\n                return\n        for cid in children[node_id]:\n            prune(cid)\n    \n    prune(root_id)\n    \n    all_node_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes]\n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': [nid for nid in all_node_ids if nid in removed],\n        'retained_node_ids': [nid for nid in all_node_ids if nid not in removed]\n    }\n```\n\nThis looks correct to me. Let me trace through case 000:\n\nnodes: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6\nchildren: root→[n1,n2,n3], n1→[n6], n2→[n4], n3→[n5], n4→[], n5→[], n6→[]\n\nprune(root):\n- root is not removable → recurse into children\n  prune(n1):\n  - n1 is not removable → recurse into children\n    prune(n6):\n    - n6 is removable, all_removable(n6) = True (leaf, removable)\n    - subtree = {n6}\n    - required_symbols_covered_outside({n6}): root covered by root, s1 covered by n1 → True\n    - removed.add(n6) → removed = {n6}\n  prune(n2):\n  - n2 is removable, all_removable(n2): n2 removable, children=[n4], all_removable(n4): n4 removable, no children → True. So all_removable(n2) = True\n  - subtree = {n2, n4}\n  - required_symbols_covered_outside({n2, n4}): root covered by root, s1 covered by n1 → True\n  - removed.update({n2, n4}) → removed = {n6, n2, n4}\n  prune(n3):\n  - n3 is not removable → recurse into children\n    prune(n5):\n    - n5 is not removable → recurse into children (none)\n\nFinal: removed = {n2, n4, n6}\nremoved_node_ids = [n2, n4, n6] (in order of original list)\nretained_node_ids = [root, n1, n3, n5]\n\nThis looks right! Let me now encode this as JSON string properly.\n\nActually, I realize I should double-check my `all_removable` logic. For n2: n2.removable=True, children=[n4], n4.removable=True, n4 has no children → all_removable(n4)=True → all_removable(n2)=True. ✓\n\nThe code looks good. Let me write the final implementation.\n\nOne edge case: the `all_removable` function with no children → returns True (since `all()` of empty is True), but we also check `node_map[node_id]['removable']` first. So for a non-removable leaf, it returns False. For a removable leaf, `all(... for cid in [])` = True, and the node itself is removable, so returns True. ✓\n\nNow let me encode the Python as a JSON string:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n        else:\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\n    \n    def subtree_ids(node_id):\n        result = [node_id]\n        for cid in children[node_id]:\n            result.extend(subtree_ids(cid))\n        return result\n    \n    def all_removable(node_id):\n        if not node_map[node_id]['removable']:\n            return False\n        return all(all_removable(cid) for cid in children[node_id])\n    \n    def covered_outside(subtree_set):\n        for sym in required_symbols:\n            if not any(n['symbol'] == sym and n['id'] not in subtree_set for n in nodes):\n                return False\n        return True\n    \n    removed = set()\n    \n    def prune(node_id):\n        if node_map[node_id]['removable'] and all_removable(node_id):\n            sub = set(subtree_ids(node_id))\n            if covered_outside(sub):\n                removed.update(sub)\n                return\n        for cid in children[node_id]:\n            prune(cid)\n    \n    prune(root_id)\n    \n    all_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes]\n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': [nid for nid in all_ids if nid in removed],\n        'retained_node_ids': [nid for nid in all_ids if nid not in removed]\n    }\n```\n\nNow I need to JSON-encode this. Let me write it out carefully with proper escaping.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\\n\\n    def subtree_ids(node_id):\\n        result = [node_id]\\n        for cid in children[node_id]:\\n            result.extend(subtree_ids(cid))\\n        return result\\n\\n    def all_removable(node_id):\\n        if not node_map[node_id]['removable']:\\n            return False\\n        return all(all_removable(cid) for cid in children[node_id])\\n\\n    def covered_outside(subtree_set):\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            if not any(n['symbol'] == sym and n['id'] not in subtree_set for n in nodes):\\n                return False\\n        return True\\n\\n    removed = set()\\n\\n    def prune(node_id):\\n        if node_map[node_id]['removable'] and all_removable(node_id):\\n            sub = set(subtree_ids(node_id))\\n            if covered_outside(sub):\\n                removed.update(sub)\\n                return\\n        for cid in children[node_id]:\\n            prune(cid)\\n\\n    prune(root_id)\\n\\n    all_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes]\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': [nid for nid in all_ids if nid in removed],\\n        'retained_node_ids': [nid for nid in all_ids if nid not in removed]\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01xhtCyt5cEOb1N9GjhdGBf4","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2207},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2207,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":73,"output_tokens":7029,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":6585},"service_tier":"standard"}}